/*Q21. Write a Java program that removes duplicate characters from a given string and forms a new string containing only distinct characters.
If a character appears multiple times, keep only its first occurrence and remove all subsequent occurrences.

The comparison of characters is case-sensitive. Therefore, uppercase and lowercase characters are considered different. For example, A and
a are treated as two distinct characters.
Input format:

• The input contains a single line containing a string that may include alphabets, digits, spaces, or special characters.
Output format:

• Print the resulting string containing only the distinct characters, while preserving the original order of their first occurrences.
Constraints:

• The input string contains at least 1 character.

• The input may contain uppercase and lowercase letters, digits, spaces, or special characters.

• Character comparison is case-sensitive.
*/
import java.util.Scanner;
 
public class DistinctChars {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);
        String s = sc.nextLine();
        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();
        for (char c : s.toCharArray()) {
            if (sb.indexOf(String.valueOf(c)) == -1) {   // keep only first occurrence
                sb.append(c);
            }
        }
        System.out.println(sb);
        sc.close();
    }
}
